Store each chunk as a single PyTorch tensor with shape [num_trajectories, trajectory_length,
    observation_dim]; axis 0 indexes trajectories, axis 1 is time, axis 2 is the flattened observation.
  - Use integer-encoded observations (dtype torch.int64); training casts to float32, so no normalization
    is needed, but downstream configs must know the observation dimension.
  - All .pkl files live in one directory; ContrastiveDataset just iterates over every file ending in .pkl
    and cloudpickle.loads it. No extra metadata is required.
  - Ensure your model/config input size matches the observation dimension (e.g., edit
    LNDenseNet.input_size if it isn’t 54). Swap in the correct env/problem generator bindings if you plan
    to train/eval beyond Rubik’s.
  - Each file is read wholly into RAM, shuffled, then batched, so keep individual pickle sizes within your
    memory budget (Rubik shards are ~2.2 GB from bs = 2048*128; lower bs or split tensors further if
    needed).
